from lib import *
C=10;S='maths'
CH='trigonometry'
lesson(C,S,CH,'trigonometric-ratios-and-identities',"Trigonometric ratios of an acute angle and identities","The flagpole","Standing 20 m from a flagpole you look up at its top at 45 degrees. How tall is it? Trigonometry links angles to lengths in a right triangle.",
["In a right triangle, for an acute angle A: sin A = opposite/hypotenuse, cos A = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan A = opposite/adjacent. @ sin, cos, tan",
 "Their reciprocals are cosec A = 1/sin A, sec A = 1/cos A, cot A = 1/tan A. @ cosec, sec, cot",
 "Also tan A = sin A / cos A. @ tan = sin/cos",
 "The basic identity is sin squared A + cos squared A = 1. @ sin^2 A + cos^2 A = 1",
 "Dividing it gives 1 + tan squared A = sec squared A and 1 + cot squared A = cosec squared A. @ 1 + tan^2 A = sec^2 A",
 "Values of special angles: sin 30 = 1/2, sin 45 = 1/root 2, sin 60 = root 3 / 2, cos is the reverse, and tan 45 = 1. @ special angles",
 "Complementary angles: sin (90 - A) = cos A and tan (90 - A) = cot A. @ complementary angles"],
("If sin A = 3/5 and A is acute, find cos A and tan A.",[("Use sin squared A + cos squared A = 1: cos squared A = 1 - 9/25 = 16/25.","The identity links sin and cos."),("cos A = 4/5 because A is acute, so cos is positive.","Take the positive root."),("tan A = sin A / cos A = (3/5)/(4/5) = 3/4.","Divide.")]),
("Define sin, cos and tan and write the main identity.",["Opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse","sin squared + cos squared = 1","Complementary angle relations"],["Mixing up opposite and adjacent"]),
["sin = opp/hyp, cos = adj/hyp, tan = opp/adj.","sin squared A + cos squared A = 1.","sin(90 - A) = cos A.","tan 45 = 1, sin 30 = 1/2, cos 60 = 1/2."],
["n|sin 30 degrees equals (decimal)|0.5|Standard value 1/2.",
 "m|tan A equals|sin A / cos A|cos A / sin A~That is cot A;sin A x cos A~Not correct|Standard identity.",
 "t|sin squared A + cos squared A = 1 for every angle A.|True|It is the Pythagorean identity.",
 "n|If sin A = 5/13 (A acute), the value of cos A times 13 is|12|cos A = 12/13.",
 "n|tan 45 degrees =|1|Opposite = adjacent.",
 "t|sin (90 - A) = cos A.|True|Complementary angle relation.",
 "n|cos 60 degrees equals (decimal)|0.5|Standard value 1/2.",
 "s|1/sin A is called ____ A.|cosec|Reciprocal of sine."],
svg=(tri([(80,250),(380,250),(380,60)],['A','B','C'],'<rect x="360" y="230" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="#333"/><text x="220" y="272" font-size="14">adjacent</text><text x="390" y="160" font-size="14">opposite</text><text x="200" y="130" font-size="14">hypotenuse</text>'),"Right triangle with sides opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse","Sides for angle A"))
lesson(C,S,CH,'heights-and-distances',"Heights and distances","Measuring the tower","You stand 30 m from a tower and see its top at 60 degrees above your eye level. The angle of elevation and a right triangle give the tower's height without climbing it.",
["The angle of elevation is the angle between the horizontal and the line of sight upwards to an object. @ angle of elevation",
 "The angle of depression is the angle between the horizontal and the line of sight downwards. @ angle of depression",
 "Draw a clear right triangle, mark the known side and angle, and choose the ratio that connects them. @ draw and mark",
 "If you know the base and want the height, use tan: height = base x tan of the angle. @ tan = height/base",
 "For the tower: height = 30 x tan 60 = 30 x root 3, about 51.96 m. @ 30 root 3",
 "The angle of elevation from A to B equals the angle of depression from B to A, because they are alternate angles. @ alternate angles",
 "Remember to add your own eye height if the problem asks for the total height. @ add eye height"],
("A ladder 10 m long makes an angle of 30 degrees with the ground. How high does it reach?",[("The ladder is the hypotenuse, the height is the side opposite the 30 degree angle.","Draw the right triangle."),("sin 30 = height / 10, so height = 10 x 1/2.","Use sin because we know the hypotenuse."),("Height = 5 m.","sin 30 = 0.5.")]),
("How do you choose between sin, cos and tan in a height-and-distance problem?",["Identify the known and unknown sides","tan for opposite and adjacent","sin or cos when the hypotenuse is involved"],["Using the wrong angle"]),
["Angle of elevation is measured up from the horizontal; depression is down.","Draw the right triangle first.","tan = opposite/adjacent; sin and cos involve the hypotenuse.","Angle of elevation = angle of depression (alternate angles)."],
["m|Angle of elevation is measured from the|horizontal|vertical~That would be 90 minus it;ground slope~Always from the horizontal line|Standard definition.",
 "n|A pole casts a 10 m shadow when the sun's elevation is 45 degrees. The pole's height (m) is|10|tan 45 = 1.",
 "t|Angle of depression from A to B equals angle of elevation from B to A.|True|Alternate angles.",
 "n|Ladder 8 m, angle with ground 30 degrees; the height reached (m) is|4|8 x sin 30 = 4.",
 "n|At 30 m from a tower the elevation is 45 degrees. Height (m) is|30|30 x tan 45.",
 "t|tan of an angle is height divided by horizontal distance.|True|Opposite over adjacent.",
 "t|The angle of elevation is measured from the vertical.|False|It is from the horizontal.",
 "s|The angle looking downwards from the horizontal is the angle of ____.|depression|Definition."])
CH='mensuration'
lesson(C,S,CH,'surface-area-and-volume-of-solids',"Surface area and volume: cylinder, cone, sphere","Making an ice-cream cone","A cone of ice-cream with a hemisphere on top: how much ice-cream fits, and how much wafer covers it? Surface area and volume formulae answer both.",
["Cylinder of radius r and height h: curved surface area = 2 pi r h, total surface area = 2 pi r (r + h), volume = pi r squared h. @ cylinder",
 "Cone of radius r, height h and slant height l (l squared = r squared + h squared): curved area = pi r l, total = pi r (l + r), volume = 1/3 pi r squared h. @ cone",
 "Sphere of radius r: surface area = 4 pi r squared and volume = 4/3 pi r cubed. @ sphere",
 "Hemisphere: curved surface area 2 pi r squared, total surface area 3 pi r squared, volume 2/3 pi r cubed. @ hemisphere",
 "For combined solids, add or subtract the parts that are visible or filled; surfaces that are joined together are not counted. @ combined solids",
 "If a solid is melted and recast, its volume stays the same, so volume before = volume after. @ volume conserved",
 "Take pi = 22/7 unless the problem says otherwise. @ pi = 22/7"],
("Find the volume of a cone of radius 7 cm and height 12 cm (pi = 22/7).",[("Volume = 1/3 pi r squared h.","Cone formula."),("= 1/3 x 22/7 x 49 x 12 = 22 x 7 x 4.","Cancel: 49/7 = 7 and 12/3 = 4."),("= 616 cubic cm.","22 x 28 = 616.")]),
("Write the formulae for the curved surface area and volume of a cylinder, cone and sphere.",["2 pi r h and pi r squared h","pi r l and 1/3 pi r squared h","4 pi r squared and 4/3 pi r cubed"],["Using height instead of slant height for the cone surface"]),
["Cylinder: CSA 2 pi r h, V = pi r squared h.","Cone: CSA pi r l, V = 1/3 pi r squared h.","Sphere: SA 4 pi r squared, V = 4/3 pi r cubed.","Melting and recasting keeps the volume the same."],
["m|The volume of a cylinder is|pi r squared h|2 pi r h~That is the curved area;1/3 pi r squared h~That is a cone|Base area times height.",
 "n|Volume (cubic cm) of a cylinder with r = 7 and h = 10, using pi = 22/7, is|1540|22/7 x 49 x 10.",
 "t|Volume of a cone is one-third of the cylinder with the same base and height.|True|Standard result.",
 "n|Surface area of a sphere of radius 7 cm (pi = 22/7) in square cm is|616|4 x 22/7 x 49.",
 "n|A cone has r = 3 and h = 4. Its slant height is|5|9 + 16 = 25.",
 "t|When a solid is melted and recast, its volume changes.|False|Volume is conserved.",
 "n|Curved surface area of a cylinder with r = 7, h = 5 (pi = 22/7) is|220|2 x 22/7 x 7 x 5.",
 "s|The slanting length of a cone is its ____ height.|slant|Definition."])
CH='statistics-and-probability'
lesson(C,S,CH,'measures-of-dispersion',"Range, variance and standard deviation","Two cricketers","Two batsmen both average 50 runs, but one scores steadily while the other swings between 0 and 100. The mean alone cannot tell them apart - we need a measure of spread.",
["Range = largest value - smallest value. It is quick but depends only on two numbers. @ range",
 "Variance measures how far values are on average from the mean: variance = sum of (x - mean) squared / n. @ variance",
 "Standard deviation is the square root of the variance, written sigma. It has the same unit as the data. @ standard deviation",
 "Shortcut: variance = (sum of x squared)/n - (mean) squared. @ shortcut formula",
 "A small standard deviation means the data are close to the mean; a large one means they are spread out. @ small or large sd",
 "If every value is increased by the same number, the standard deviation does not change; if every value is multiplied by k, it is multiplied by k. @ effect of change",
 "Coefficient of variation = (sigma / mean) x 100 percent, used to compare the consistency of two data sets. @ coefficient of variation"],
("Find the standard deviation of 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.",[("Mean = 30/5 = 6.","Add and divide."),("Squared deviations: 16, 4, 0, 4, 16, sum 40; variance = 40/5 = 8.","Square each (x - 6)."),("Standard deviation = root 8, about 2.83.","Take the square root of the variance.")]),
("Why do we need the standard deviation as well as the mean?",["Mean shows the centre not the spread","SD measures spread around the mean","Lower SD means more consistent"],["Thinking a larger mean means larger spread"]),
["Range = max - min.","Variance = average squared deviation; SD = its square root.","Adding a constant leaves SD unchanged.","Lower coefficient of variation = more consistent."],
["n|Range of 3, 8, 5, 12, 6 is|9|12 - 3 = 9.",
 "m|Standard deviation is the|square root of variance|square of variance~That is the opposite;mean of variance~Not a definition|By definition.",
 "t|Adding 5 to every value changes the standard deviation.|False|Spread stays the same.",
 "n|Variance of 1, 3, 5 (mean 3) is (decimal)|2.67|(4 + 0 + 4)/3 = 2.67.",
 "t|A smaller standard deviation means the data are more spread out.|False|It means they are closer to the mean.",
 "n|If every value is doubled, the standard deviation is multiplied by|2|SD scales with the multiplier.",
 "n|Mean 50, SD 5. Coefficient of variation in percent is|10|5/50 x 100.",
 "s|The square root of variance is the ____ deviation.|standard|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,CH,'probability-and-addition-theorem',"Probability: sample space, events and the addition theorem","Tossing coins","Toss a coin and a die together. Which outcomes are possible, and what is the chance of a head with an even number? Probability counts favourable outcomes against all outcomes.",
["The sample space S is the set of all possible outcomes of an experiment, and an event is a subset of S. @ sample space",
 "Probability of event A = n(A)/n(S), where all outcomes are equally likely. @ P(A) = n(A)/n(S)",
 "The probability of any event lies between 0 and 1; a sure event has P = 1 and an impossible event P = 0. @ 0 to 1",
 "The complement of A, written A', has P(A') = 1 - P(A). @ complement",
 "Addition theorem: for any two events A and B, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B). @ addition theorem",
 "If A and B are mutually exclusive (cannot occur together), P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). @ mutually exclusive",
 "Throwing a die: S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, so P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2. @ die"],
("A die is rolled. Find the probability of getting a number less than 3 or an odd number.",[("A = {1, 2}, B = {1, 3, 5}, and A and B = {1}.","List the events."),("P(A or B) = 2/6 + 3/6 - 1/6.","Addition theorem."),("= 4/6 = 2/3.","Check by listing: {1, 2, 3, 5} has 4 outcomes.")]),
("State the addition theorem of probability.",["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)","Subtract the overlap","Simplifies for mutually exclusive events"],["Forgetting to subtract the overlap"]),
["P(A) = favourable / total.","0 is less than or equal to P(A) which is less than or equal to 1.","P(not A) = 1 - P(A).","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)."],
["n|How many outcomes on a die are even numbers?|3|2, 4 and 6.",
 "t|A probability can be 1.5.|False|Probability lies between 0 and 1.",
 "n|P(rain) = 0.3. P(no rain) is|0.7|1 - 0.3.",
 "m|For mutually exclusive events P(A or B) =|P(A) + P(B)|P(A) x P(B)~That is for independent events;P(A) - P(B)~Not correct|They have no overlap.",
 "n|Number of outcomes when two coins are tossed|4|HH, HT, TH, TT.",
 "t|The probability of a sure event is 1.|True|By definition.",
 "n|P(A) = 0.4, P(B) = 0.5, P(A and B) = 0.2. P(A or B) is|0.7|0.4 + 0.5 - 0.2.",
 "s|The set of all possible outcomes is called the ____ space.|sample|Definition."])
